e read the better we get it  reading my wife didn't like to read  she'd fall asleep when she started  reading but she stuck with it and she  started reading more and more and then  she found books that interested her it  happened to be books about history  historical novels and now she enjoys  reading  um she didn't like playing the piano  when she had a piano teacher but now  she's playing piano on her own and she  plays more and more my granddaughter  granddaughter had the same experience so  the more you do something you can  develop not only do you get better at it  but you can  start to like it more  uh the expression that i often quote in  french la petite  the appetite comes as we eat  so  getting back to listening some people  say they have trouble concentrating  while listening i have said that i am  able to concentrate better if i am  listening while doing something else the  dishes going for a walk and i have had  people on my youtube channel concur that  that it's difficult to sit in a chair  put some you know language content on  and just sit and listen  it seems to be easier for some people  to concentrate when we're doing  something else it is also very practical  in that we can be  doing two things at the same time we can  take advantage of what would otherwise  be dead time one of my big criticisms of  rosetta stone is that you're forced to  sit there in front of the computer  uh now something happened to me which i  want to share with you i was listening  to  this uh french chancegne george prasas  who  i enjoyed i enjoyed when i lived in  france  and it suddenly dawned on me  that here i am listening to his songs  and i am totally focused on every word  and i remember that when i lived in  france when i was bathed in french i was  a student there for three years i went  to school in french spoke french every  day every day did everything in french  uh  and i haven't really had that much  french since then occasionally once a  year i run into french-speaking people  visitors or i we have been in france on  a few occasions and so forth but when i  was most  uh immersed in french i would listen to  these french chanson singers and i  wouldn't understand the words because  it's sometimes difficult to pick up the  words  and i found that i i understood  every word  and i attribute this  to  my commitment to  language learning through listening over  the last really 10 years it's 10 years  ago that i started working on my  cantonese using a minidisc player and  it's then that i discovered the power of  these very small  and yet very you know high quality  recording devices of course the mini  disc player progressed into the mp3  player the ipod and  android and all of the things that we  have today  so i spent a lot of time listening to  cantonese  korean  portuguese  russian and now czech and i have trained  my ability to listen and to focus while  listening  when i listen to check now  i hear every word it's not a blur i  don't know all the words but i hear  every word very very clearly  so what i'm saying there is some of you  may  not like listening some of you say that  you've  tuned out  uh some of you say that you prefer to  watch movies  all of that is undoubtedly true  i would just  recommend that you make the effort to  try to  get better at listening and focusing  while you're listening it's an acquired  skill and the more you do it the better  you get at doing it and if you have that  ability it becomes a tremendous learning  tool  in my view and i've mentioned this  before they should teach that as a  learning skill at schools  i even suggested partly tongue-in-cheek  that they have kids run around the  schoolyard listening to their lessons on  an mp3 player  you know i see sometimes the efforts of  schools  or universities to have online  courses and they all want to have like  an online classroom like the teacher is  there and somehow broadcasting it out to  many many people and they can get on and  ask questions and it's all very  interactive and so forth  and there certainly is a place for that  kind of interactive  learning process  but i believe a big part of learning  you've got to do on your own whether  you're reading or listening and reading  and of course we also have to develop  the ability to read and focus on what  we're reading because reading is such a  powerful way of learning not only  learning languages but learning in  general but listening is another skill  and we should  train kids to get good at listen listen  listen to five different versions of  the uh you know  something to do with say ancient  uh egypt okay  one ten minute  story another little lecture another  whatever and you listen and listen and  you hear about the same things through  different avenues uh different  perspectives and you're listening and  and if you get good at it then you can  listen while you're walking to school  walking back from school it's probably  too ambitious in terms of school kids  but if we can train that that is a  tremendously valuable skill we can learn  while in the car and we don't fade out  we stay focused so all i'm saying here  is listening  the ability to listen and of course to  listen just as in learning languages you  need words you need vocabulary and so  the in order to increase one's  vocabulary of course the combination  of listening and reading or if you want  to take it one step further the kind of  interface and support that we have at  link and no no elsewhere can can make  sure that the vocabulary grows because  to understand what's being said you have  to know the words it's also possible to  listen and listen listen and eventually  guess and figure out some of the missing  words but it goes faster if we combine  it with reading so there you have it i  just wanted to sort of get back to this  thing about listening yeah everybody  learns in different ways some people  like watching videos  but  if you really want my advice i'm quite  convinced  that for everybody if they can train  themselves they will learn better  and they will learn more efficiently if  they can train themselves to be good  readers but beyond that good listeners  and if they are good listeners they will  become good readers so listening  is key  in terms of language learning literacy  learning in general we have the most  wonderful tools now  for listening we have the most amazing  resources available on the internet  all kinds of interesting courses and and  audio material that you can download  learn to be a good listener  and that's what i wanted to say today i  look forward to hearing from you bye
 

